   "options": {
    "A": "$\\frac{8}{3}$",
    "B": "8",
    "C": "$\\frac{77}{9}$",
    "D": "$\\frac{80}{9}$\n\nOfficial Ans. by NTA (B)\nAns. (B)\nSol. $s \\equiv \\sin t, c \\equiv \\cos t$\nLet orthocentre be (h,k)\nSince it if an equilateral triangle hence orthocentre coincides with centroid.\n$$\\begin{array}{l}\n\\therefore a+s+c=3 h, b+s-c=3 k \\\\\n\\therefore(3 h-a)^{2}+(3 k-b)^{2}=(s+c)^{2}+(s-c)^{2}=2\\left(s^{2}+c^{2}\\right)=2 \\\\\n\\therefore\\left(h-\\frac{a}{3}\\right)^{2}+\\left(K-\\frac{b}{3}\\right)^{2}=\\frac{2}{9}\n\\end{array}$$\ncircle centre at $\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{a}}{3}, \\frac{\\mathrm{~b}}{3}\\right)$\nGives, $\\frac{\\mathrm{a}}{3}=1, \\frac{\\mathrm{~b}}{3}=\\frac{1}{3} \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{a}=3, \\mathrm{~b}=1$\n$$\\therefore \\quad a^{2}-b^{2}=8$$\n8. For $\\alpha \\in \\mathrm{N}$, consider a relation R on N given by $\\mathrm{R}=\\{(\\mathrm{x}, \\mathrm{y}): 3 \\mathrm{x}+\\alpha \\mathrm{y}$ is a multiple of 7$\\} . \\quad$ The relation R is an equivalence relation if and only if :\n(A) $\\alpha=14$\n(B) $\\alpha$ is a multiple of 4\n(C) 4 is the remainder when $\\alpha$ is divided by 10\n(D) 4 is the remainder when $\\alpha$ is divided by 7\n\nOfficial Ans. by NTA (D)\nAns. (D)\nSol. For R to be reflexive $\\Rightarrow \\mathrm{x} \\mathrm{R} \\times$\n$$\\begin{array}{l}\n\\Rightarrow 3 \\mathrm{x}+\\alpha \\mathrm{x}=7 \\mathrm{x} \\Rightarrow(3+\\alpha) \\mathrm{x}=7 \\mathrm{~K} \\\\\n\\Rightarrow 3+\\alpha=7 \\lambda \\Rightarrow \\alpha=7 \\lambda-3=7 \\mathrm{~N}+4, \\mathrm{~K}, \\lambda, \\mathrm{~N} \\in \\mathrm{I} \\\\\n\\therefore \\text { when } \\alpha \\text { divided by } 7, \\text { remainder is } 4 .\n\\end{array}$$\n\nR to be symmetric xRy ⇒ yRx\n$$\\begin{array}{l}\n3 \\mathrm{x}+\\alpha \\mathrm{y}=7 \\mathrm{~N}_{1}, 3 \\mathrm{y}+\\alpha \\mathrm{x}=7 \\mathrm{~N}_{2} \\\\\n\\Rightarrow(3+\\alpha)(\\mathrm{x}+\\mathrm{y})=7\\left(\\mathrm{~N}_{1}+\\mathrm{N}_{2}\\right)=7 \\mathrm{~N}_{3}\n\\end{array}$$\n\nWhich holds when $3+\\alpha$ is multiple of 7\n$\\therefore \\alpha=7 \\mathrm{~N}+4$ (as did earlier)\nR to be transitive\n$$\\begin{array}{l}\nx R y \\& y R z \\Rightarrow x R z \\\\\n3 x+\\alpha y=7 N_{1} \\& 3 y+\\alpha z=7 N_{2} \\\\\n3 x+\\alpha z=7 N_{3} \\\\\n\\therefore 3 x+7 N_{2}-3 y=7 N_{3} \\\\\n\\therefore 7 N_{1}-\\alpha y+7 N_{2}-3 y=7 N_{3} \\\\\n\\therefore 7\\left(N_{1}+N_{2}\\right)-(3+\\alpha) y=7 N_{3} \\\\\n\\therefore(3+\\alpha) y=7 N\n\\end{array}$$\n\nWhich is true again when $3+\\alpha$ divisible by 7, i.e. when $\\alpha$ divided by 7 , remainder is 4 .\n9. Out of 60\\% female and 40\\% male candidates appearing in an exam, 60\\% candidates qualify it. The number of females qualifying the exam is twice the number of males qualifying it. A candidate is randomly chosen from the qualified candidates. The probability, that the chosen candidate is a female, is :\n(A) $\\frac{3}{4}$\n(B) $\\frac{11}{16}$\n(C) $\\frac{23}{32}$\n(D) $\\frac{13}{16}$\n\nOfficial Ans. by NTA (A)\nAns. (Bonus)\nSol.\n\n20 male qualified\n40 female qualified\n\nProbability that chosen candidate is female $=\\frac{40}{60}=\\frac{2}{3}$\n\nJEE Exam Solution\nwww.esaral.com\n3"
   },
